1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,300
 Hosting ASP.NET Core App in a Docker container.

2
00:00:05,300 --> 00:00:14,400
 It's time we are all waiting for to host an ASP.NET Core App in a Docker container.

3
00:00:14,400 --> 00:00:21,300
 This is an important topic, so make sure you concentrate on what I am doing.

4
00:00:21,300 --> 00:00:27,600
 Also, you should do this side-by-side on your PC with me.

5
00:00:27,600 --> 00:00:37,800
 This way, you will learn it more quickly, open Visual Studio.

6
00:00:37,800 --> 00:00:41,000
 Click create a new project.

7
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:49,600
 Next, select deed ASP.NET Core Web App model view controller template.

8
00:00:49,600 --> 00:00:57,100
 Name the project something like First Docker App.

9
00:00:57,100 --> 00:01:02,200
 Then choose the location on your PC where you want to save this app.

10
00:01:02,200 --> 00:01:09,400
 There is a checkbox that says place solution and project in the same directory.

11
00:01:09,400 --> 00:01:12,100
 You need to check this option.

12
00:01:12,100 --> 00:01:20,900
 This is very necessary because Docker needs to find the .NET App files on the saved location.

13
00:01:20,900 --> 00:01:29,200
 If you uncheck this option, then Docker will not be able to find the .NET App files

14
00:01:29,200 --> 00:01:32,600
 and it will fail to create a container.

15
00:01:32,600 --> 00:01:36,600
 Click next.

16
00:01:36,600 --> 00:01:39,600
 Here, select the framework.

17
00:01:39,600 --> 00:01:42,800
 I am selecting latest .NET 10.

18
00:01:42,800 --> 00:01:47,800
 Here, see the checkbox saying enable container support.

19
00:01:47,800 --> 00:01:54,800
 You can check this option if you want to host the app from the Docker container from the beginning.

20
00:01:54,800 --> 00:02:03,200
 You can also enable container support, which is Docker after the app is created.

21
00:02:03,200 --> 00:02:12,800
 I will keep this option unchecked because I want to show how you can add Docker container support to apps

22
00:02:12,800 --> 00:02:15,800
 that are already functioning.

23
00:02:15,800 --> 00:02:19,400
 This will help you to learn Docker in greater depth.

24
00:02:19,400 --> 00:02:21,700
 Now click the create button.

25
00:02:21,700 --> 00:02:25,300
 This will create the .NET App.

26
00:02:25,300 --> 00:02:35,500
 The app is created.

27
00:02:35,500 --> 00:02:37,800
 Let us run it.

28
00:02:45,800 --> 00:03:00,300
 It is just a basic app containing only two pages, home and privacy.

29
00:03:00,300 --> 00:03:04,800
 Close the browser and the console window.

30
00:03:04,800 --> 00:03:11,800
 In the Docker desktop, you can see I have no images and no containers right now.

31
00:03:15,800 --> 00:03:25,800
 Now, on Visual Studio, let's add Docker container support.

32
00:03:25,800 --> 00:03:32,800
 So, right click on the project name, select add menu option.

33
00:03:32,800 --> 00:03:37,800
 Then you will see the option saying container support.

34
00:03:37,800 --> 00:03:43,800
 Click on this option, which will add Docker container support to the .NET App.

35
00:03:46,800 --> 00:03:51,800
 In new window opens where you can select container options.

36
00:03:51,800 --> 00:03:56,800
 On the container OS, select line X container build type.

37
00:03:56,800 --> 00:03:59,800
 Let it remain Docker file.

38
00:03:59,800 --> 00:04:02,800
 Container image distro.

39
00:04:02,800 --> 00:04:08,800
 You can select the line X version like Ubuntu, Debian, Alpine, etc.

40
00:04:08,800 --> 00:04:11,800
 Let it also remain default.

41
00:04:11,800 --> 00:04:16,800
 For container build context, let it remain as it is.

42
00:04:16,800 --> 00:04:19,800
 Next, click the ok button.

43
00:04:19,800 --> 00:04:23,800
 Visual Studio has added Docker support to the app.

44
00:04:23,800 --> 00:04:27,800
 And Docker file is created for this app.

45
00:04:27,800 --> 00:04:30,800
 Notice on the left bottom corner.

46
00:04:30,800 --> 00:04:34,800
 Visual Studio is now performing some task.

47
00:04:34,800 --> 00:04:39,800
 It is actually creating the Docker image of this app.

48
00:04:39,800 --> 00:04:45,800
 And also creating a line X based Docker container that will host this app's image.

49
00:04:45,800 --> 00:04:49,800
 Now, the task is completed.

50
00:04:49,800 --> 00:04:57,800
 If you open Docker desktop, you will now see two images created on the Docker server.

51
00:04:57,800 --> 00:05:00,800
 The first one is tsp.net.

52
00:05:00,800 --> 00:05:06,800
 10.0 image which is downloaded from Azure container registry.

53
00:05:06,800 --> 00:05:11,800
 This registry is also known as Microsoft container registry.

54
00:05:11,800 --> 00:05:14,800
 Shortform MCR.

55
00:05:14,800 --> 00:05:18,800
 Second image is the apps image which Docker has created.

56
00:05:18,800 --> 00:05:22,800
 The image name is kept same as the apps name.

57
00:05:22,800 --> 00:05:28,800
 Now go to the container section where you will find a new container.

58
00:05:28,800 --> 00:05:31,800
 Having the name first Docker app.

59
00:05:31,800 --> 00:05:37,800
 This container is actually hosting our.net apps image.

60
00:05:37,800 --> 00:05:42,800
 Visual Studio has lots of inbuilt Docker features.

61
00:05:42,800 --> 00:05:46,800
 And I should explain them to you now.

62
00:05:46,800 --> 00:05:51,800
 On the output window select container tools.

63
00:06:01,800 --> 00:06:07,800
 Here you will see the different task which Docker has performed.

64
00:06:07,800 --> 00:06:23,800
 Like pulling.net 10.0 image from MCR starting containers, creating image layers and setting the whole image of the.net app.

65
00:06:31,800 --> 00:06:47,800
 Finally, there is a Docker run command which is creating the Docker container for running the.net apps image.

66
00:06:47,800 --> 00:07:05,800
 On visual studio me know you will also find a new container Docker file option which will enable you to start the Docker container from visual studio itself.

67
00:07:05,800 --> 00:07:08,800
 Click this option to run the app.

68
00:07:17,800 --> 00:07:44,800
 Visual Studio opens the container window showing the container and image of this app.

69
00:07:44,800 --> 00:07:55,800
 Congratulations you can now see the app is hosted from a Docker container and it's working correctly.

70
00:07:55,800 --> 00:08:01,800
 Also, you will see environment variables.

71
00:08:01,800 --> 00:08:20,800
 Ports, volumes, logs, etc.

72
00:08:20,800 --> 00:08:41,800
 Actually, it has switched all the details from Docker desktop.

73
00:08:41,800 --> 00:08:54,800
 Now open Docker desktop then click the container name.

74
00:08:54,800 --> 00:09:14,800
 You will see the logs, files, stats and other informations which you already saw on visual studio.

75
00:09:14,800 --> 00:09:28,800
 On the exe tab you can run line X commands like ls which list all the files and folders inside the container.

76
00:09:28,800 --> 00:09:35,800
 Also find the ports of the host machine and container ports.

77
00:09:35,800 --> 00:09:48,800
 These ports are actually listed on the Docker file.

